# Ably realtime — https://ably.com/accounts
# Server-only key. Never expose a full-capability key to the browser; the client
# mints short-lived, room-scoped tokens through /api/ably/token instead.
# A room needs this: unlike Giphy there is no offline stand-in for other people.
ABLY_API_KEY=

# Play over BroadcastChannel instead of Ably — one browser, many tabs, no
# network. What the test suite runs on, so it needs no credentials. The
# ?transport=broadcast URL lever does the same thing for one page load.
ABLY_STUB=

# The LAN address this machine serves on, for testing with a real second
# device. Read only by `next.config.mjs`, and only by `next dev` — Next blocks
# cross-origin requests to its dev assets, so a phone on the same wifi is
# refused its chunks without this. Find it with `ipconfig getifaddr en0`, and
# leave it unset the rest of the time.
LAN_HOST=

# Base URL used to build the room join link encoded in the QR code.
#
# Leave it unset in development. Unset, the QR encodes whatever origin the host
# is actually on — including the LAN address a phone can reach. Set to
# localhost, it encodes a link that is dead on every device but the one that
# drew it, which is exactly the case a QR code exists for.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=

# Giphy — https://developers.giphy.com/dashboard/
#
# PUBLIC, and it has to be. Giphy's terms forbid proxying their API — every
# call must come from the browser — so this ships in the client bundle and
# anyone can read it. That is Giphy's own model: keys are rate-limited, not
# secret, and they ask you to issue a separate one per platform for exactly
# this reason. Use a web-only key here and rotate it if it gets abused.
#
# The allowance is 100 calls an hour. That number used to size the whole room —
# ten players, five rounds, three searches each — and no longer does: Klipy is
# the default and its production key is unmetered. See ADR-0020 and ADR-0026.
# Giphy stays as a second adapter, and on this allowance it is a fallback for a
# small room rather than a way to run one.
#
# Without a key the picker serves offline sample art in development, so a
# fresh clone still has a working board.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GIPHY_API_KEY=

# Klipy — the second GIF provider, and the one the app is moving to.
#
# PUBLIC for the same reason Giphy's is: Klipy's integration requirements also
# forbid proxying the API and caching the media without written approval, so
# every call comes from the browser. What switching bought is the allowance, not
# the architecture — a *production* key is free and unmetered, and it is what
# every cap in the room is now sized against (ADR-0026).
#
# **Use a production key here.** A Klipy **test** key is 100 calls an hour — the
# same ceiling as the Giphy beta key — and a full room will outrun it. It
# reports what is left in `x-ratelimit-remaining`, which the browser cannot
# read, so `lib/gifs/usage.ts` counts locally instead; the read-out is on
# /components.
#
# Get one at https://partner.klipy.com. See ADR-0022.
NEXT_PUBLIC_KLIPY_API_KEY=

# Which provider the picker asks. `klipy` or `giphy`; unset takes the built-in
# default. Naming a provider with no key falls through to one that has a key
# rather than erroring, so a clone holding either key gets a live board.
# The ?gifs=giphy / ?gifs=klipy URL levers do the same for one page load.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GIF_PROVIDER=

# Force that sample art even when a key is present, to keep a long session of
# layout work off the rate limit. The ?gifs=stub URL lever does the same thing
# for one page load.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GIFS_STUB=

# Open this many guest tabs when you open a room, so one room can be watched
# from several seats at once. Development only — a production build ignores it,
# and so does a hand-typed ?auto= on the join URL.
#
# Not the same thing as ?bots=N. Bots live inside the host's tab, so there is
# nothing to look at: no second viewport, no guest's own chat rail, no second
# picker spending its own search budget. These are real tabs, each its own peer.
#
# Each tab keeps the nickname it was already suggesting and joins on a stagger,
# because the person record is one localStorage entry every tab shares. Blank
# or 0 opens none; 6 is the cap.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_GUESTS=

# Anthropic — https://platform.claude.com
#
# SERVER-ONLY, and the only key in this app that is. Both GIF keys ship to the
# browser because their providers forbid a proxy (ADR-0020, ADR-0022); nothing
# forbids one here, so nothing justifies handing a model key to twenty
# browsers. That asymmetry is the entire reason `app/api/bots/turn/route.ts`
# exists after ADR-0020 deleted the last proxy route.
#
# An ungated route that proxies a model is a free-token faucet, so the route
# refuses any request without a signed seat — the same boundary
# `/api/ably/token` uses, minted by the same secret.
#
# **Do not use a personal key.** Issue it from a project-owned organisation,
# scoped to a workspace that carries a monthly spend limit *and* a rate limit:
# a spend limit is monthly and cannot notice a loop that burns the budget in an
# hour. A service-account key rather than a personal one, which is archived
# when its owner leaves the org. See ADR-0035.
#
# Without a key, bots play with written-in jokes in development, the way a
# keyless picker still draws a board. In production a missing key is an error.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Force those written-in jokes even when a key is present, to keep a long
# session of layout work off the bill. The ?brain=stub URL lever does the same
# thing for one page load, and ?brain=live turns it back off.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BOTS_STUB=
